<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swedish Academy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1786">1786</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Swedish Academy was founded by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iii">Gustav III</a> in 1786 to promote the purity and strength of the Swedish language, on the model of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a>. It has eighteen members elected for life and publishes a great dictionary and the official word list of Swedish. Since 1901 it has chosen the winners of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swedish-academy.jpg" alt="Swedish Academy" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Swedish Academy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svenska_Academien_seal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The statutes of the Swedish Academy were drawn up by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iii">Gustav III</a> himself. Its motto is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">Talent</a> and Taste (Snille och smak), and its stated primary purpose is to work on the purity, strength and sublimity of the Swedish language. The king assigned it the task of compiling a dictionary, set annual competitions in oratory and poetry on given themes and ordered a medal struck every year.</p><p>It is said that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iii">Gustav III</a> first intended twenty members, half the number of the French Academy, but chose eighteen because the Swedish expression De Aderton, The Eighteen, had such a fine solemn ring. After his death in 1792 the Academy's standing deteriorated. In 1794 the member Gustav Mauritz Armfelt was excluded after a sentence for treason, and in 1795 the Academy was suspended for two years on political grounds. Armfelt was reinstated in 1805 and excluded again in 1811.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Members are elected by secret ballot, and the result must be submitted to the Academy's Patron, the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, for approval. The members include writers, linguists, literary scholars, historians and a jurist. Writers were at first a minority, but during the twentieth century they grew to more than half of The Eighteen. Membership was held for life, and the Academy could not accept a resignation, though it could exclude a member. In 1989 Kerstin Ekman and Lars Gyllensten stopped taking part in its work after it declined to condemn the death decree issued against Salman Rushdie, and the number of active members fell.</p><p>The Academy has met in the building now known as the Stockholm Stock Exchange Building since its founding. Its ballroom was then the largest room in Stockholm that could be heated in winter, and Gustav asked to borrow it. The Academy gained permanent use of the upper floor only in 1914.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Academy carries out its mission through reference works. Svenska Akademiens ordlista is a one-volume spelling dictionary. Svenska Akademiens Ordbok, edited on principles similar to those of the Oxford English Dictionary, runs to 39 volumes, the first published in 1893 and the last in 2023. A four-volume grammar, Svenska Akademiens grammatik, is aimed at researchers, linguists and students. Since 1901 the Academy has chosen the winner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature, a task it accepted after some initial hesitation. Modernisation followed, including the election of its first female member, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selma-lagerlof">Selma Lagerlöf</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swedish Academy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
